    "created_at": "2026-04-14T19:14:39Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-04-14T19:14:39Z",
    "body": "Thanks for raising this issue. This is/was one of the bigger pains in the #!@$ of the existing algorithms. Generally, I consider all of these cases to be authoring errors - you shouldn't be trying to open or close popovers from within one of the events triggered by opening or closing popovers. Thanks for finding more corner cases!\n\nTo me at least, the most important thing is that we add console errors for these cases (not necessarily in the spec, but in implementations) so that developers know they're holding it wrong. But then the browser should just do the easiest thing with the fewest chances for further unintended consequences. Given that, I agree with your comment:\n\n> I feel like option 1 is the right answer here. I think I'd just set a number on the document like \"popover hiding depth\" which increments at the start of a hiding operation, and decrements after it. If this is > 0 and there's a request to show a `auto` or `hide` popover, it throws.\n\nI like this idea. And I'm quite afraid of option 3, since it requires carefully thinking through all of the algorithms to see what happens when there's a rogue popover in one of the stacks.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
